Bound dashed stroke expansion per SVG render.

Telegram-originated hardening, not an upstream backport. It requires
qtbase_5.15.19/0051-bound-dash-expansion-per-render.patch, whose private
QDashBudget lets QtGui charge the dash loop iterations of QDashStroker
and of the raster engine's dashed lines, and the outline geometry their
dashes add, to one pair of allowances. QtGui alone limits dash
repetitions only per segment or per line, so an SVG repeating such
segments over many paths and <use> references could emit hundreds of
millions of dashes from a few kilobytes.

Both QSvgTinyDocument::draw() overloads now arm one QDashBudget of 65536
iterations and 2^20 geometry units after their early returns. All
painting on that thread until the draw returns spends those allowances,
and a draw nested in it adds none. A dashed segment or line that no
longer fits is drawn solid, as at Qt's repetition limit; the qtbase
patch describes the units and the rarer skips. Qt 5's QtSvg has no
trusted source option, draws no filters, masks or patterns and never
paints while computing bounds, so these two draws are its only rendering
entry points.

diff --git a/src/svg/qsvgtinydocument.cpp b/src/svg/qsvgtinydocument.cpp
